      "message": "kernel: Call log callbacks without holding the logger mutex\n\nRelease KernelLogger::m_mutex while a log callback runs, so a callback\ncan log, or destroy kernel objects that log, without deadlocking.\nPreviously Log() held m_mutex across every callback, so an entry logged\nfrom inside a callback waited for the mutex its own thread held. A\ngarbage collector running finalizers inside a callback triggers this: a\nfinalizer that destroys a chainstate manager flushes and logs, and the\nprocess hangs. See\nhttps://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/34775#issuecomment-6278257737\nand https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/34374#discussion_r4030551866.\nCreating or destroying another connection from inside a callback hung\nthe same way.\n\nMultiple connections and the API are unchanged. Log() walks the list\nof callbacks holding m_mutex only to step to the next one, and each\ncallback has a count of running invocations. Destroying a connection\nmarks its callback removed so no new invocations start, waits for the\ncount to reach zero, and then destroys the callback and its user_data\noutside m_mutex, so it still waits for invocations on other threads.\nThe count is only decremented and notified under m_mutex, and destroy\ntakes m_mutex again before freeing the callback, so Log() never touches\na freed callback. Destroying a connection from inside its own callback\nwould wait for itself, so it asserts instead.\n\nBehavior change: callbacks can run on several threads at the same time\nand must be thread-safe, and entries from different threads have no\nglobal order. The header documents this and which kernel calls are\nsafe from inside a callback. bitcoin-chainstate and the test logger now\nwrite each line in one call so concurrent lines don't interleave.\n\nNew test_kernel cases, which all hang without this change: a callback\nthat logs and destroys a chainstate manager and context, a callback\nthat destroys another connection, and destroying a connection while\ninvocations run on other threads.\n\nThis change was written with Claude Opus 5.5.",
