      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "body": "Closing in favor of #36768, which fixes the same deadlock by delivering log entries through `btcsignals::signal` instead of this PR's hand-written callback list, providing a simpler implementation and a more straightforward nonblocking interface.\r\n\r\nBoth PRs keep the current API, including multiple connections, and run callbacks without holding the logger mutex, so a callback can log and destroy kernel objects. Both have tests showing it no longer hangs. The difference is what destroying a connection does while its callback is running on another thread:\r\n\r\n- This PR waits for the call to return, like the current code and [#34775](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/34775). A thread waiting for its own call can't make progress, so a callback must not destroy a connection whose callback is running on the same thread. This PR asserts when a callback destroys its own connection.\r\n- [#36768](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/36768) doesn't wait, like boost::signals2 and the node's other btcsignals users. The connection's user data is destroyed when the running call returns. A callback can destroy any connection, including its own. Callers that need to wait can do it from the user data's destroy callback.\r\n\r\n#36768 also needs less kernel code (`bitcoinkernel.cpp` +42/-77 vs. +45/-16 here), since the signal implementation is shared.",
